I want you to build a web app called Mewakir.
It should work like a simple version of Google Meet — people can create meetings, join them, and talk over video and audio in real time.

The app should have two main parts:

A backend that handles users, meetings, and live connections.

A frontend website that people use to log in, start, or join a meeting.

Here’s what I want it to do:

When a user opens the site, they first type their name to continue.

On the home screen, they can either create a new meeting or join an existing one using a meeting ID and password.

When a meeting is created, the app should generate a random meeting ID and password.

If two people join the same meeting, they should be able to see and hear each other using their cameras and microphones.

The video should appear side-by-side, one for “you” and one for the “other person.”

There should be buttons to mute/unmute and turn the camera on/off.

It should feel simple, modern, and clean — similar to Google Meet’s design, but lightweight.

The backend should:

Keep a list of meetings (like a simple database).

Allow creating and joining meetings.

Handle real-time communication so browsers can exchange video and audio directly.

Be reliable, secure, and easy to run locally.

The frontend should:

Use modern web tools and be responsive.

Have three pages: login, home, and meeting.

Use the browser’s camera and microphone safely and give clear messages if permissions are denied.

Automatically connect to others in the same meeting so the video works right away.

The goal is not fancy design or lots of features — just a working foundation where people can meet each other face-to-face through the browser.
After that, we can add things like chat, screen sharing, and better user accounts later.



React, GO and MONGODB
